      "textSnapshot": "Problem: Search result ranking and retrieval for prose documents had five interconnected heuristic failures: file classification was halving scores for ordinary source files with prose-like names; prose sections were being collapsed back into file-level units; prose-promotion byte budgeting was not accounting for funded blocks already in use; enclosure-margin requests were being silently ignored for short callables; and section labels were causing responses to exceed their byte budgets because labels were assigned after budget fitting.\nSolution:\nChanged the prose unit of retrieval from file to section by introducing proseSection tracking on candidates and attachProseSectionUnits(), which keys each region to the heading it falls under. This prevents markdown documents from collapsing to a single result per file.\nFixed file classification to reject documentation prefixes only when the file has no known language, preventing license_check.go, history_store.py, and similar source files from being mislabeled.\nRemoved \"expected\" from fixture intent terms—the most common word in bug reports (\"expected X, got Y\")—which was switching the fixture prior off on ordinary defect queries.\nAdded searchExpressionBodiedCallable() to recognize Kotlin/C#/Scala expression-bodied functions (= expr, =\u003e expr) as implementations rather than declarations, preventing real fix sites from being demoted.\nApplied enclosure margin padding before the \"already complete\" short-circuit, so --enclosure-context-lines now works for rank-1 results whose snippet already spans the body.\nMoved section-label assignment to ranking time (before fitting), charging the ~28-byte label cost to the fitter instead of exceeding budget after allocation.\nModified expandProseResolution() to accept and respect a reserved parameter for bytes spent by other funded blocks (type cards, signature types), preventing multi-resolution promotion from overspending the shared ceiling.\nRefined prose query detection to require other than as an adjacent phrase (not independent words) and to validate bounded plural noun frames after are, preventing singular questions like \"what are we doing about deployments\" from being misread as list requests.\nAdded negation prefixes no and not to prevent compound identifiers like NotEncrypted from matching evidence for their opposite.\nAll fixes are covered by a new 375-line test file validating the corrections.",
